<h2>When to update your baseline</h2>

<p>If you are training consistently, your threshold will move. You need to re-test every two to three months. However, there are specific physiological signals that indicate you should test earlier.</p>

<p><strong>Low RPE and negative decoupling:</strong> Decoupling (Pa:HR) measures the relationship between your pace and your heart rate over time. If you run 4 x 8 minutes at your prescribed threshold pace, and your heart rate remains entirely flat across the final reps without drifting upward, your aerobic efficiency has improved. If this low decoupling is paired with a noticeable drop in perceived exertion, your zones are stale. It is time to re-test.</p>

<p><strong>Returning from an interruption:</strong> If you miss training due to illness, travel, or irregular weeks, do not attempt to pick up where you left off. The methodology requires two "comeback weeks" consisting exclusively of easy Zone 1 and Zone 2 running, plus light neuromuscular activations. After those 14 days, you execute a new 30-minute field test. Expect a lower threshold number, reset your zones, and start building again.</p>

<h2>The case for power, and why you likely don't need it</h2>

<p>There is a valid counter-argument to relying solely on pace, heart rate, and RPE. Wearables like Stryd are objectively superior when terrain becomes a variable.</p>

<p>Pace is useless on rolling hills. Heart rate lags too far behind short, punchy gradients. Running power meters normalize the effort, allowing you to hold a steady physiological output regardless of the incline. For athletes using advanced software like WKO5, power data feeds complex models that calculate mFTP, Functional Reserve Capacity (FRC), and Time to Exhaustion (TTE). If your power curve plateaus, we can insert dedicated max-effort probes into your week to fix the model and refine your exact capabilities.</p>

<p>If you race on highly variable terrain, power is the priority metric. It removes the guesswork.</p>

<p>But if you are a budget-conscious amateur running on flat roads, canal paths, or a local track, the environmental variables are minimal. You do not need an advanced physics model to tell you that you are running hard. By maintaining strict discipline in your easy runs, executing a proper 30-minute field test, and listening to the hard biological marker of your own breathing rate, you can execute a Norwegian threshold block flawlessly.</p>
